====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|with definite article}} Something absent, especially absent [[people]] collectively; those who were or are not there. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* '''1772''', {{w|Frances Burney}}, ''Journals & Letters'', Penguin 2001, 30 May:
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1947|author=w:Cecil Day Lewis|title=Poetic Image
|passage=That very sense of longing, of yearning for the '''absent''', which 'nostalgia' conveys to us now.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|Scotland}} An [[absentee]]; a person who is not there. {{defdate|15th–19th c.}}<ref name=DSL>{{R:DSL|pos=n}}</ref>

====Preposition====
{{wikidata lexeme|L11790}}
{{en-head|prep}}

# In the absence of; [[without]]; except. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 20<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{ux|en|'''Absent''' taxes modern governments cannot function.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2011|author=David Elstein|magazine=London Review of Books|volume=33|issue=15|passage=the Princess Caroline case {{...}} established that – '''absent''' a measurable ‘public interest’ in publication – she was safe from being photographed while out shopping.}}
#* {{RQ:Atlantic|author=Ian Bogost|title=I tried to limit my screen time (It didn't go well)|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2019/09/why-apple-screen-time-mostly-makes-things-worse/597397/|date=2019-09-05|passage=And the distraction-management software Freedom offers a mode that won’t unlock affected apps '''absent''' a telephone-support call. |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260601120431/https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2019/09/why-apple-screen-time-mostly-makes-things-worse/597397/|archivedate=1 June 2026}}

