===Etymology 2===
{{wikidata lexeme|L330737}}
From {{inh|en|enm|absenten}}, from {{der|en|fro|absenter}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|absento|absentāre|t=keep away, be away}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/æbˈsɛnt/|a=RP}}, {{enPR|ăbsĕnt'}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-absent (verb).wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/æbˈsɛnt/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛnt|s=2}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{anchor|absentfrom}}{{lb|en|reflexive}} To keep (oneself) away.
#: {{ux|en|Most of the men are retired, jobless, or have otherwise temporarily '''absented''' themselves from the workplace.}}
#* {{RQ:Orwell Animal Farm|6
|passage=This work was strictly voluntary, but any animal who '''absented''' himself from it would have his rations reduced by half.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To keep (someone) away. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=IX|passage=Go; for thy stay, not free, '''absents''' thee more;|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} Stay away; withdraw. {{defdate|Attested from around 1350 to 1470 until the late 18<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Douglass Bondage|passage=The iron rule of the plantation, always passionately and violently enforced in that neighborhood, makes flogging the penalty of failing to be in the field before sunrise in the morning, unless special permission be given to the '''absenting''' slave.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} Leave. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To [[omit]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|page=188|chapter=Reflections on Eisenstein and Digital Imagery: Of Mice and Men|author=Paul Willemen|title=The Montage Principle||text=When we realize that the digitalisation of the image involves - by reducing and even '''absenting''' - the role of 'the physical', the 'sensuous' (by reducing and eventually '''absenting''' the indexical aspects of the image) {{...}}}}

